::ENG3_U03_PRE_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.R.05] Passage: An essay begins with a problem, gives two examples, compares solutions, and ends with a recommendation. What structure is used? {
~A random list.#The parts are organized.
=Problem, examples, comparison, and recommendation.#Correct. This names the structure.
~Definition only.#The essay does more than define.
~Personal timeline.#No life timeline is described.
}

::ENG3_U03_PRE_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.R.06] Passage: Citizens, a law no one can understand cannot guide anyone fairly. What is the central idea? {
~Unclear law is fair.#The passage says it cannot guide fairly.
~Citizens should avoid laws.#The passage focuses on understanding law.
=Law must be understandable to be fair.#Correct. This captures the central idea.
~Guidance is unrelated to law.#The passage connects them.
}

::ENG3_U03_PRE_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.R.07] Passage: Citizens, a law no one can understand cannot guide anyone fairly. What is the author's purpose? {
~To persuade readers that unclear law is acceptable.#The author argues against unclear law.
~To explain how to build a guidepost.#Guide is figurative.
~To entertain readers with a story.#No story is told.
=To persuade the audience that law should be clear.#Correct. The author argues for clarity.
}

::ENG3_U03_PRE_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Passage: Argument A says the city should publish minutes because residents missed decisions. Argument B says the city should livestream meetings because residents missed decisions. What shared information appears? {
=Residents missed decisions.#Correct. Both arguments use this detail.
~Residents attended every vote.#The passage says they missed decisions.
~The city has no meetings.#Meetings are discussed.
~Livestreaming already happened.#It is proposed.
}

::ENG3_U03_PRE_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Passage: Argument A says publish minutes; Argument B says livestream meetings. Both use the fact that residents missed decisions. How do the ends differ? {
~A supports secrecy, while B supports cancellation.#Neither supports that.
=A supports written access, while B supports live access.#Correct. The same fact supports different access methods.
~Both support the same exact solution.#The solutions differ.
~Neither uses evidence.#Both use missed decisions as evidence.
}

::ENG3_U03_PRE_Q06::[MLA.ENG3.R.12] Passage: Truth is not a whisper for locked rooms; it is a bell for the square. What rhetorical choice appears? {
~A statistic.#No number appears.
~A recipe.#No steps are given.
=Contrasting metaphors.#Correct. Whisper and bell contrast private secrecy with public truth.
~A direct legal citation.#No law is cited.
}

::ENG3_U03_PRE_Q07::[MLA.ENG3.R.12] Passage: Truth is not a whisper for locked rooms; it is a bell for the square. What effect does the rhetoric create? {
~It makes truth seem private and hidden.#The bell image suggests public truth.
~It removes the author's purpose.#The contrast clarifies purpose.
~It proves bells are legal records.#The bell is figurative.
=It makes public truth seem clear and shared.#Correct. The bell image supports openness.
}

::ENG3_U03_PRE_Q08::[MLA.ENG3.R.10] Passage: A hidden rule is a trap, not a law. Which paraphrase is most accurate? {
=Rules that people cannot see or understand are unfair.#Correct. This captures the meaning.
~All laws are traps.#The passage criticizes hidden rules specifically.
~Traps should become laws.#This reverses the meaning.
~Rules are never needed.#The passage supports fair rules.
}

::ENG3_U03_PRE_Q09::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Which sentence uses academic vocabulary most precisely? {
~The author does stuff with words.#This is vague.
~The passage is good because it sounds good.#This is imprecise.
=The author uses contrast to emphasize the difference between public truth and secrecy.#Correct. This uses precise academic vocabulary.
~The thing in the text is nice.#This is vague.
}

::ENG3_U03_PRE_Q10::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which sentence is conventionally correct? {
~The author uses contrast it supports the purpose.#This is a run-on.
~the author uses contrast to support the purpose.#The first word should be capitalized.
~The author, uses contrast to support the purpose.#The comma incorrectly separates subject and verb.
=The author uses contrast to support the purpose.#Correct. The sentence follows standard conventions.
}
